\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {18}{\ignorespaces Solver-gap stability of the decomposition. At the loose tolerance the original solves used, the small topology and interaction terms sat within solver noise; only at a $0.01$\,\% gap do they resolve to stable values. Loss dominance is far above the gap throughout.}}{26}{table.caption.35}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:gapstability}{{18}{26}{Solver-gap stability of the decomposition. At the loose tolerance the original solves used, the small topology and interaction terms sat within solver noise; only at a $0.01$\,\% gap do they resolve to stable values. Loss dominance is far above the gap throughout}{table.caption.35}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Supply-temperature flexibility. Lowering the plant supply temperature reduces thermal loss but shrinks the temperature difference, so flow and pumping rise. The cost-optimal reduction is 17.5\,K; beyond 20\,K the pipe velocity limit binds, and hydraulics change from a negligible cost into the binding constraint.}}{26}{figure.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:tsup}{{8}{26}{Supply-temperature flexibility. Lowering the plant supply temperature reduces thermal loss but shrinks the temperature difference, so flow and pumping rise. The cost-optimal reduction is 17.5\,K; beyond 20\,K the pipe velocity limit binds, and hydraulics change from a negligible cost into the binding constraint}{figure.8}{}}
